.Dd September 29, 2026
.Dt NEWSYSLOG.CONF 5
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm newsyslog.conf
.Nd log rotation configuration
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The file
.Pa /etc/newsyslog.conf
tells
.Xr newsyslog 8
which logs to rotate, when, and how.
Each line describes one log, in fields separated by white space:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
logfile [owner:group] mode count size when [flags [pid_file [signal]]]
.Ed
.Pp
A
.Ql #
starts a comment, which runs to the end of the line.
A line
.Ql include Ar path
.Po or
.Ql <include> Ar path
.Pc
reads another file, each file of a directory whose name ends in
.Pa .conf ,
in name order, or each file a shell pattern matches.
A path that does not exist is ignored.
.Pp
The fields are:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Ar logfile
The log's absolute path name; with the
.Cm G
flag, a shell pattern, every match of which is a log.
The name
.Ql <default>
gives the settings for files named on the
.Xr newsyslog 8
command line that no other line names.
.It Ar owner : Ns Ar group
Optional: the owner and group of the new log, as names or numbers.
Either may be left out, keeping the old file's.
.It Ar mode
The new log's mode, in octal.
.It Ar count
How many archives to keep.
With 0, a rotated log is removed, not archived.
.It Ar size
The size, in kilobytes, at which the log is rotated, or
.Ql *
not to rotate by size.
.It Ar when
When the log is rotated regardless of its size:
.Ql * ,
never; a number, that many hours after the last rotation; or a rotation time,
optionally after a number of hours.
A log is rotated when its size or its time comes, whichever is first.
A rotation time is either
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Cm @ Ns Oo Oo Oo Oo Ar cc Oc Ns Ar yy Oc Ns Ar mm Oc Ns Ar dd Oc Ns Oo Cm T Ns Oo Ar hh Ns Oo Ar mm Ns Oo Ar ss Oc Oc Oc Oc
An ISO 8601 time.
Fields before the first one given mean
.Dq every ,
fields after the last one given are zero:
.Ql @T00
is every midnight,
.Ql @01T05
the first of each month at 05:00, and
.Ql @0101T
every New Year.
.It Cm $ Ns Oo Cm W Ns Ar weekday | Cm M Ns Ar day Oc Ns Op Cm D Ns Ar hh
A day, week or month:
.Cm D Ns Ar hh
is the hour (0 if left out),
.Cm W Ns Ar weekday
the day of the week, 0 being Sunday, and
.Cm M Ns Ar day
the day of the month, or
.Cm ML
its last day.
.Ql $D0
is every midnight,
.Ql $W0D23
Sundays at 23:00, and
.Ql $M1D0
the first of each month.
.El
.Pp
A log with a rotation time that has never been rotated is rotated only
within the hour that time names.
An interval counts from the last rotation; a log never rotated is rotated
once it has something in it.
.It Ar flags
Optional letters, or
.Ql -
for none:
.Bl -tag -width 3n -compact
.It Cm B
The log is binary: write no rotation message into the new file.
.It Cm C
Create the log if it is missing, when
.Xr newsyslog 8
is run with
.Fl C .
.It Cm D
No dump.
Accepted, and does nothing.
.It Cm G
The
.Ar logfile
field is a shell pattern.
.It Cm J
Compress archives with
.Xr bzip2 1 .
.It Cm N
Signal no process.
.It Cm R
Run the command in the
.Ar pid_file
field, instead of signalling a process.
.It Cm U
The pid file names a process group, which is signalled.
.It Cm X
Compress with
.Xr xz 1 ,
when it is installed.
.It Cm Y
Compress with
.Xr zstd 1 ,
when it is installed.
.It Cm Z
Compress archives with
.Xr gzip 1 .
.El
.It Ar pid_file
Optional: the file holding the process ID of the daemon to signal after the
log is rotated; with
.Cm R ,
the command to run.
The default is
.Pa /var/run/syslog.pid .
.It Ar signal
Optional: the signal, as a number or a name such as
.Dv SIGUSR1 ;
the default is
.Dv SIGHUP .
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width ".Pa /usr/local/etc/newsyslog.conf.d" -compact
.It Pa /etc/newsyslog.conf
the configuration
.It Pa /etc/newsyslog.conf.d
more configuration, for the system
.It Pa /usr/local/etc/newsyslog.conf.d
more configuration, for installed software
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Keep five compressed weeks of a daemon's log, rotated on Sundays, and tell
the daemon with
.Dv SIGUSR1 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
/var/log/food.log  daemon:  640  5  *  $W0D0  JC  /var/run/food.pid  SIGUSR1
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr newsyslog 8 ,
.Xr syslogd 8
.Sh HISTORY
The format is
.Fx Ns 's .
